WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.530
Film Courage: Have you ever pitched to one
of the studios?

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:05.879
David Willis, screenwriter/producer: Yeah,
I pitched to one of the indie studios once

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:08.389
a particular script, I mean I pitched to them.

00:00:08.389 --> 00:00:15.259
But this one particular indie producer, I
was brought into a meeting to pitch to him

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:20.710
by another producer (who was a woman) and
she says “Come meet this guy and we’ll

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:21.890
pitch him this movie.”

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:26.789
So I came in, I pitch him the movie, and it
was a romantic comedy and the male character

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:31.179
(the lead character) starts out with one girlfriend,
breaks up with her and ends up with another

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:32.830
girlfriend by the end of the movie.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:38.770
And the reason I bring that up is because
to pitch it (this guy, this executive) he

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:41.020
says “So is that what your life is like?”

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.020
“What do you mean?”

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.140
“Well this guy, he’s got all these women
in your movie.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:45.260
He’s got all these women!

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:46.260
What the hell is going on?”

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.300
And I go “He has one girlfriend and he gets
another one.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.060
I don’t think that’s really a lot?”

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.580
And he says “I don’t know, that sounds
like a lot to me.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.790
He seems like a womanizer, that’s what he
seems like his life is like to me.”

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:58.940
And I’m like “Oh, okay?”

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.450
This anger was coming at me.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.620
And I’m like it’s just a weird meeting.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.962
Anyway, so we leave and the woman producer
that I came with she goes “I apologize.”

00:01:05.962 --> 00:01:07.720
And I go “What’s going on?”

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:13.611
And she goes “Well…I’ve been dating
him and he wants to be exclusive and I don’t

00:01:13.611 --> 00:01:16.610
want to be exclusive…”

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:25.250
So all this guy was doing was sounding off
at me as a way of telling her you know “Why

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.750
won’t you me exclusive to me?”

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.780
That’s really what the meddle was about.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:37.140
That was the weirdest pitch meeting ever because
it had nothing to do with me, you know?

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.620
And some times it’s out of your control.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:39.620
What can you do?

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:43.840
You just do you job, pitch, pitch, pitch,
smile, do your job and that’s it.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.200
Film Courage: At least she was there to tell
you that because you wouldn’t have known

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.620
otherwise.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.210
You would have thought it was you.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.630
David: And I was not dating her by the way.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:53.920
Film Courage: No, no, no.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:54.920
I get, I get it.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.659
But at least someone was there to tell you
because so many times things like that happen

00:01:57.659 --> 00:01:59.860
and there is any for it and we’ll never
know!

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.740
But at least you got to know that.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:06.070
So when you when in to do other things [pitch
other projects] that experience was always

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:10.780
in the back of your mind and maybe it’s
totally not about me, but I don’t have that

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:15.140
woman there telling me the story as to why
someone is acting this way?

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.700
David: One of the first pitches I did, I came
in with a pretty famous writer.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:22.970
We’d written something together and we were
pitching at Paramount.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:30.100
And as we were pitching, he was talking and
we had this long, super complicated, really

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.790
detailed pitch that we thought was wonderful,
he was describing it.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:40.410
And as we’re pitching, as we’re telling
the story, the guy (this executive) he starts

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:45.780
to do this [David Willis imitates a man falling
asleep].

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.540
Film Courage: Oh no.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:53.690
David: And after awhile we realized “We’re
putting him to sleep!”

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:54.940
Film Courage: Oh no!

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:59.099
David: We’re putting him to sleep!

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:05.770
And you know, we’re comedy writers so we
stopped and we started talking about it but

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:06.770
what a note to get!

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.820
“You’re putting me to sleep.”

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:14.170
We actually, literally, this guy and so that
was like a huge lesson for us…we learned

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:15.190
keep the pitch short.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:18.430
We went into all these frickin’ details,
you know?

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:21.400
So we’ll never forget that one.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:22.400
Film Courage: Yeah.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.680
Well, there was no note-behind-the-note.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.790
You knew what was going on with that one,
okay?

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:27.310
David: Indeed.

